Emacsvox News -- History Of User-Visible Changes

Maintained by Emacsvox Contributors.

* Emacsvox 60 --- Current User-Visible Changes

This version requires Emacs 31 or later.

** Native Windows Speech Under WSL

Emacsvox now owns its native Windows speech stack.  The bundled
~windows-outloud~ and ~windows-dtk~ servers provide Eloquence and Software
DECtalk through native Windows bridges, while ~windows-play~ provides ordered,
interruptible auditory icons.  Main and notification speech can use separate
native streams with configurable stereo positions.

Use ~make windows-speech~ to build the complete stack, or the
~windows-audio~, ~windows-outloud~, and ~windows-dtk~ targets separately.
Select a server with ~M-x tts-select-server~, configure native auditory icons
with ~emacsvox-windows-speech-configure-audio~, and inspect dependencies with
~emacsvox-windows-speech-diagnose~.  Proprietary Eloquence files and the
separately licensed DECtalk runtime are not stored in the repository.
Replaceable navigation transactions are discarded when newer input is
waiting, and adjacent formatting runs are now batched into one native
utterance per auditory-icon-delimited segment.  This prevents rapid movement
from leaving obsolete synthesis work queued after the user stops.

** Omnivox Logical Voices And Replaceable Presentations

The Omnivox adapter now negotiates its bounded, versioned control channel and
discovers structured engine, capability, and physical-voice inventory without
injecting server events into speech.  It registers Emacsvox logical voices and
normalized ACSS styles atomically with both main and notification processes.
Registration waits for each inventory response.  An unconfigured logical voice
uses that process's advertised preferred engine, while explicit portable
selectors continue to resolve against its own installed voices.

On Windows, Omnivox now initializes WinRT and eSpeak together when available.
WinRT remains the default preferred engine; ~OMNIVOX_ENGINE=espeak~ changes the
preference without hiding WinRT from discovery or fallback.

Customize ~omnivox-logical-voice-preferences~ to give a logical voice ordered
exact, engine-default, or property selectors.  Exact selectors retain separate
engine and native voice IDs; property selectors resolve against each machine's
actual inventory.  Missing engines and voices degrade through configured
alternatives, and unresolved definitions remain visible in
~omnivox-logical-voice-registration~.  Run
~omnivox-register-logical-voices~ after changing preferences at runtime.
Semantic preference keys such as ~voice-bolden~ follow their current generated
ACSS voice binding, so users do not need to discover or hard-code generated
voice symbols.

Omnivox voice changes now carry their logical voice ID into the legacy queue.
Servers advertising ~logical_voice_routing~ use the registered engine and
physical voice for following queued spans, while missing bindings fall back to
the preferred engine.  Older servers ignore the additive directive and retain
their existing pitch behavior.

Current Emacsvox uses ~presentation_timeline_v3~ for complete structured Aural
delivery.  Timelines carry an explicit ordered, replaceable, or urgent policy;
large payloads use bounded multipart framing and are validated before
admission.  A newer valid replaceable timeline immediately cancels queued,
synthesizing, and buffered output only in its matching replacement domain.
Ordered, urgent, legacy, and differently keyed output remain intact.  The
reader's short coalescing delay applies only to matching replaceable work;
ordered and urgent timelines execute immediately.  ~emacsvox_tx~ remains the
bounded legacy fallback for a server without timeline v3.

Queued logical voices now re-resolve after a runtime missing-voice,
unavailable-engine, or synthesis failure and retry the identical chunk on the
configured fallback.  Each chunk is limited to four attempts, and stop
generation checks prevent cancelled speech from starting a fallback attempt.
Runtime failures also feed persistent engine health circuits.  Cooldown and a
single recovery probe keep ordinary requests on healthy fallbacks until the
preferred engine recovers.  Immediate speech and isolated-letter commands use
the current global routing policy and health snapshot, but do not carry a
named logical voice.

** TTS Prefix Map Rename

The generic speech prefix map is now ~emacsvox-tts-submap~.  The former
~emacsvox-dtk-submap~ name has been removed without a compatibility alias.
User configuration that binds or inspects that map must use the new name.
The default _C-e d_ key sequence is unchanged.

** Aural Presentation

Emacsvox semantic aural presentation can combine speech, device-independent
voices, auditory cues, pauses, and portable stereo placement before or after
content.  A fixed internal compatibility baseline preserves existing voices
and icons.  Users compose ordered Presentation Options above it and can add
stronger personal, session, or buffer overrides.  Org's optional heading-level
labels, section cues, and visibility announcements are Presentation Options.

The =emacsvox-show-point= line marker is now configurable through
=emacsvox-show-point-presentation=.  Use
=M-x emacsvox-set-show-point-presentation= to preview animated voice,
micro-tone, earcon, combined, spoken, custom-rule, and silent styles.  A prefix
argument also changes the global default.  Non-voice markers are placed at the
exact point boundary; the =custom= style exposes registered point facts for
personal presentation rules.

Capitalization and indentation indication now separate enablement from
presentation.  =C-e d c= and =C-e d i= remain the master toggles;
=C-e d C= selects spoken, tone, combined, custom-rule, or silent
capitalization, while =C-e d I= selects spoken indentation, a duration-coded
tone, a fixed-duration rising-pitch tone, either spoken combination, custom
rules, or silence.  Prefixing either selector also changes its global default.
Queued capitalization and indentation retain their source text and publish
semantic boundary facts for the Aural Presentation cascade.  Omnivox
negotiates the same capitalization selection for isolated letters.  The
rising indentation tone increases pitch with indentation and is never shorter
than the registered blank-line tone.  Indentation tones now complete before
line speech instead of clipping against its first phoneme.

Sentence and clause chunking no longer repeats
an object or run boundary action within one formatting run, and blank or
whitespace-only visual lines no longer stack a physical-line boundary earcon
on their existing line-condition feedback.  The native Windows Eloquence
server now drains preceding speech before starting an external point earcon,
so the separate speech and cue playback queues preserve its text boundary.
Omnivox now discards an interrupted timeline's unfinished overlay tail before
rendering a replacement line, preventing a cancelled point earcon from
reappearing at the beginning of the next navigation utterance.  The
Presentation Profiles manager now labels its voice-provider column
=Voice Palette=.  The voice and earcon remap-at-point workflows explicitly
speak their prepared result after completion selection instead of relying on
buffer-local message speech.
Vertico's initial file completion presentation now speaks the minibuffer prompt
and current directory exactly once as one ordered utterance.  Later candidate
navigation speaks only the changed candidate, while preserving the initial
directory from pronunciation suppression.
The selected presentation profile is now applied completely at startup rather
than restoring only its identity and option list.  Diverged profiles name the
differing setting areas and direct users to press =v= for saved-versus-live
details.
Aural Home no longer duplicates the visual-face policy or buffer-local Voice
Lock toggles.  The advanced face-rule policy remains available through
=M-x emacsvox-aural-toggle-face-presentation= and the Aural Doctor; use
=C-e d v= for Voice Lock in the current buffer and =C-e d C-v= globally.
Presentation profiles no longer capture, apply, display, or compare
buffer-local Voice Lock.  Older saved compatibility fields remain readable
and are discarded when registered, so subsequent profile saves omit them.
Profiles now use the fixed internal compatibility baseline rather than saving
or displaying a selectable scheme.  Older =:scheme= profile fields remain
readable and are discarded during registration.  Aural Home no longer shows
a Schemes route; its Presentation Options and Presentation Overrides routes
are the user-facing composition controls.
The six selectable Org demonstration schemes have been retired.  Their useful
user choices remain available through the existing Org Presentation Options,
without replacing the compatibility baseline.
Normal startup and builds no longer load the retired scheme manager or simple
scheme editor, and their interactive list, selection, and edit commands are no
longer exposed.  The declarative rule editor remains available for personal
Presentation Options and presentation overrides.
Personal data schema 7 retires the =:schemes= field.  Existing version 1
through 6 files remain readable; migration discards only their local scheme
definitions while preserving Presentation Options, voice palettes, profiles,
selected profile identity, and personal rules.  New saves no longer publish
scheme state, and applying a profile no longer changes it.
The semantic vocabulary list now provides titled-cell and boundary navigation.
Preferred interactive command aliases share the discoverable
~emacsvox-aural-~ prefix; established verb-first names remain compatible.
Any number of independently enabled Presentation Options can add speech, cues,
or styling without copying or replacing the compatibility baseline, while
persistent personal rules remain stronger.
Use ~emacsvox-aural-list-feature-fragments~ for the spoken presentation-option
manager.  Its normal view groups options into expandable integration
collections; press ~a~ for enabled options in exact weakest-to-strongest
order.  Toggling preserves an option's stable precedence.  The manager
supports viewing, creating, copying, editing, deleting, validating, toggling,
and explicitly reordering options; all changes save atomically.
Press ~P~ to preview an option without changing its enabled state; the normal
preview composes it with the active configuration, while ~C-u P~ plays the
option alone.  A matching object in the remembered source buffer is previewed
with its live facts and mode.  Otherwise the manager clearly identifies a
simulation and offers representative examples supplied by the integration,
with safe selector-derived examples for rules the provider did not cover.
Org supplies heading-level and folded-heading examples.  Notmuch supplies
unread-result and attachment examples for the shared mail-status option.
Data-only integration definitions now use the
~emacsvox-aural-provider-SCOPE.el~ naming convention.  Org, Markdown,
Notmuch, and shared workflow providers are available at startup without
loading their external packages; the corresponding ~emacsvox-SCOPE.el~
libraries retain live hooks, fact capture, advice, and speech commands.
The former internal libraries ~emacsvox-aural-org~,
~emacsvox-aural-markdown~, and ~emacsvox-aural-representative~ have been
replaced by the provider names without compatibility aliases.
Optional built-in Org options independently add a heading-level label, a
section cue, or folded/opened feedback.  Speech actions may safely substitute
registered semantic values with templates such as ~Heading {level}~; every
field must be guaranteed by the selector, and Emacs converts the result to
literal text before queueing it.  Org headings now always expose the
registered ~visibility~ attribute as ~folded~ or ~expanded~, while preserving
the older folded state for compatible rules.
~emacsvox-explain-aural-presentation~ reports the facts and winning rules at
point.  It infers an occasion with matching rules, speaks the resolved
before/content/after order, and accepts a prefix argument to choose an
occasion explicitly.  Existing icon calls, personality properties, face
mappings, and sound directories remain supported through compatibility
adapters.
Personal rules can now match a named visual face with the ~:legacy-face~
selector and combine an appended or prepended cue with a palette voice.
Ordered face lists remain layered: all matching rules may contribute actions,
the strongest face breaks otherwise tied scalar voice settings, and semantic
identity remains preferred within the same origin.  The Presentation Option
and override editors can create and edit face presentations, and explanation
reports the captured faces strongest first.
Interactive Up and Down line movement now submits the same ~navigation~ and
~focus-entered~ semantics as structural movement commands.  Arriving on an
Org heading with an arrow key therefore uses the same heading presentation as
~org-next-visible-heading~; continuous-reading commands remain ~continuous~.
Notmuch search buffers explicitly keep Up and Down equivalent to ~C-n~ and
~C-p~; the existing ~n~ and ~p~ thread-navigation commands are unchanged.
Use ~emacsvox-aural-list-profiles~ to save and switch complete named
combinations of ordered Presentation Options, sound pack, voice palette, and
portable spatial settings.  Profiles reference existing
components instead of copying their rules, and activation is transactional.
Exactly one persisted profile identity is selected.  It is shown as ~active~
while live settings match its saved values and as ~diverged~ after the
presentation-option order, sound, voice, or spatial settings change.  In the
profile manager, ~w~ writes current live values into a saved profile, while
~a~ applies saved values and confirms before replacing divergence.  Other
profiles remain inactive even when they happen to contain identical values.
Personal data schema version 7 stores this selected identity and retires local
scheme definitions; older files migrate automatically.
Use ~emacsvox-aural-doctor~ for a spoken report of live bindings, loaded source
or byte-code, active presentation, resources, personal data, spatial fallback,
and speech-server state.  Repairs are explicit and limited to deterministic
safe operations.
Notmuch, Gnus, Dired, and Magit now submit registered workflow facts around
their existing speech and auditory cues.  Presentation Options and overrides
can therefore distinguish mail status, filesystem state, and version-control
sections without changing the default feedback.
Their remaining legacy cue paths are now fully enclosed by semantic
presentation boundaries, including message parts, attachments, thread
boundaries, group operations, filesystem inspection, Magit views, blame,
diffs, and process completion.
Agent Shell is now an end-to-end reference integration for transcript
content, session lifecycle, tools, permissions, source blocks, tables,
viewports, and prompt editing.  Its chat overlays are normalized before speech,
so decorative status glyphs and button names do not leak to another engine.
Semantic item content uses the selected voice, collapsed or expanded state is
announced after the item, upward and downward visual-line navigation agree,
and an empty editable prompt says =Me. Ready for input.=  Ordinary Dired next
and previous line movement now speaks the entry without a redundant
=select-object= earcon; marking and other state-changing operations retain
their cues.  Markdown now exposes headings, tasks, lists, links, code blocks,
tables, footnotes, visibility, and document operations, and line movement uses
the same semantic heading intent as structural navigation.  A source audit
maintains the migrated-module set and rejects new context-free direct icon
calls while allowing named compatibility adapters.

** Search From Calendar

Calendar keys =gg= and =gG= start Google searches after or before the selected
date.  Keys =gy= and =gY= start the corresponding YouTube searches through
EMPV.

** EMPV And YouTube

The =emacsvox-empv= integration speaks YouTube results and playback state.  It
also provides URL and local-file playback, media history, precise relative or
absolute seeking, current-position feedback, and audio-filter and channel
controls through EMPV's repeat map.


** Speech-enable Consult

Module ~emacsvox-consult~ speech-enables the ~consult~ package.
